A||N||1||PeteB||Looking for 87/88 GT Buying Tips||||1||
Z||000000||PeteB||07-29-2000||01:24 PM||||I was wondering if anyone could help me out...<P>I'm new to the Fiero forums here, but am trying to track down some buying faq/tips/guide information online.  If you know of any, please drop me a note in email.  I'm considering buying a '87 Fiero GT with 75,000 miles, and although I have a good background with other collector cars, I'm not sure what the 'trouble spots' are with these cars.  I hear a lot of people mentioning that 1988 models are the ones to get, but I don't see much information on why!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  If this car doesn't seem right, I'm going to keep an eye out for '87 '88 GT models, so any help would be appreciated!<P>Thanks!<BR>Pete<BR>pbartusek@aol.com ||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-29-2000||02:18 PM||||The 88 model Fieros mainly featured brake and suspension improvements, plus a higher resell value. The 87 GT's are good cars and usually can be purchased cheaper than the 88's. Lots of upgrades can be done to the earlier Fieros to make the brakes and suspension as good if not better than the 88's.<P>The typical things to watch out for is worn driver's seat, cracked lower dash corners, sagging headliners, inoperative headlights, exhaust manifold cracks, and if it's a manual shift, double check the whole clutch system. That will get you started, I'm sure others will jump in here and add things I left out.||||reg||1
